# zenblend NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench tier_isolation --features _dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
input:   1920px premultiplied RGBA f32 rows, 100k iters per arm

## Result

| op                  | NEON       | scalar     | speedup |
|---------------------|------------|------------|---------|
| blend_row/Multiply  | 2857.2 ns  | 5027.5 ns  |  1.76x  |
| lerp_row            |  872.7 ns  | 1358.7 ns  |  1.56x  |
| blend_row/SrcOver   |  881.6 ns  | 1222.9 ns  |  1.39x  |
| mask_row            |  608.4 ns  |  764.8 ns  |  1.26x  |

No regression on any path.

Worth noting: `blend_modes.rs` describes the artistic/Porter-Duff modes as
"representative scalar" next to "the SIMD-dispatched SrcOver path", but
Multiply measures 1.76x — the highest ratio here, above SrcOver's 1.39x. Those
modes are SIMD-dispatched too. The header comment in blend_modes.rs is
misleading on that point.

Contract gotcha for anyone extending this bench: `lerp_row`'s `t` and
`mask_row`'s `mask` are PER-PIXEL (width) slices, not per-component (width*4).
Passing per-component slices trips a length assert at runtime.
